{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HTTE3J4sTkELcvzXsrZqvy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# METABENCH MMLU Data Summary\n2\t\n3\t## Dataset Overview\n4\t\n5\t| Metric | Value |\n6\t|--------|-------|\n7\t| Models (CSV) | 5,227 |\n8\t| Models (RDS, after preprocessing) | 5,219 |\n9\t| Items | 12,508 |\n10\t| Subjects | 57 |\n11\t| Total response rows | 73,383,381 |\n12\t| Data format | Long format (source, item, correct), binary 0/1 |\n13\t\n14\tModels per subject ranges 5,225–5,227 (near-complete coverage). 8 models were filtered during METABENCH's preprocessing step (RDS).\n15\t\n16\t## Pre-fitted IRT Parameters (from `mmlu-preproc.rds`)\n17\t\n18\tThe RDS file contains **ready-to-use 2PL IRT parameters**:\n19\t\n20\t| Parameter | Min | Mean | Median | Max |\n21\t|-----------|-----|------|--------|-----|\n22\t| Difficulty (diff) | 0.008 | 0.444 | 0.524 | 0.689 |\n23\t| Discrimination (disc) | -0.551 | 0.076 | 0.107 | 0.291 |\n24\t| SD | 0.106 | — | — | 0.500 |\n25\t\n26\t- **0 items excluded** by METABENCH's quality filter\n27\t- **1,926 items (15.4%)** with negative discrimination — these behave inversely (higher-ability models get them wrong more often)\n28\t- **258 floor items** (empirical p < 0.05), **0 ceiling items** (p > 0.95)\n29\t- Empirical p-value range: [0.011, 0.919]\n30\t\n31\t### Train/Test Split\n32\t\n33\tMETABENCH provides a pre-built split in `mmlu-preproc-split.rds`:\n34\t- **Train**: 4,755 models (91.1%)\n35\t- **Test**: 464 models (8.9%)\n36\t\n37\t## Model Accuracy Distribution\n38\t\n39\t| Statistic | Value |\n40\t|-----------|-------|\n41\t| Mean | 0.592 |\n42\t| Std | 0.160 |\n43\t| Min | 0.218 |\n44\t| Max | 0.911 |\n45\t| P25 | 0.543 |\n46\t| Median | 0.661 |\n47\t| P75 | 0.697 |\n48\t| Below random (0.25) | 366 (7.0%) |\n49\t\n50\tStrong bimodal tendency: most models cluster in 0.54–0.71, with a long left tail of weak/broken models.\n51\t\n52\t## Cohort Analysis (for DIF / temporal grouping)\n53\t\n54\t### By Model Family Classification\n55\t\n56\tOnly **1,929 of 5,219 (37.0%)** models could be classified into known families by regex on model names. The remaining 3,290 are community fine-tunes, merges, and unrecognizable names.\n57\t\n58\t### Annual Cohorts\n59\t\n60\t| Year | N models | N ≥ 80? |\n61\t|------|----------|---------|\n62\t| 2021 | 5 | No |\n63\t| 2022 | 13 | No |\n64\t| 2023 | 1,253 | **Yes** |\n65\t| 2024 | 658 | **Yes** |\n66\t\n67\t**Two annual cohorts meet the N≥80 threshold (2023 and 2024).** However, 2024-H2 only has 2 classified models — the dataset likely predates most 2024-H2 releases. The bulk of classified 2024 models are from H1.\n68\t\n69\t### Top Families\n70\t\n71\t| Family | Count |\n72\t|--------|-------|\n73\t| mistral-0.1 | 400 |\n74\t| llama-3 | 308 |\n75\t| llama-2 | 161 |\n76\t| llama-1 | 135 |\n77\t| mixtral-8x7B | 130 |\n78\t| solar | 92 |\n79\t| zephyr | 85 |\n80\t| qwen-1.5 | 67 |\n81\t| phi-2 | 60 |\n82\t\n83\t### Alternative Cohort Strategy\n84\t\n85\tSince 63% of models are unclassified, **name-based cohort assignment is insufficient for fine-grained temporal analysis**. Consider:\n86\t1. Using accuracy-based quintiles as proxy cohorts\n87\t2. Using the RDS train/test split directly (4755/464)\n88\t3. Cross-referencing with HuggingFace Hub metadata for release dates\n89\t\n90\t## RL-trained Models\n91\t\n92\t**477 models** show RL/alignment signals in their names:\n93\t\n94\t| Method | Count |\n95\t|--------|-------|\n96\t| DPO | 346 |\n97\t| ORPO | 52 |\n98\t| PPO | 11 |\n99\t| KTO | 6 |\n100\t| RLHF | 1 |\n101\t| Reward | 1 |\n102\t\n103\tNote: these are models with explicit RL signals in names. Many models (Llama-3, Qwen-2.5, etc.) used RLHF but don't advertise it in their names. The DPO-labeled models alone provide a substantial pool for RL contamination analysis.\n104\t\n105\t## Subject Difficulty Ranking\n106\t\n107\t### Hardest (lowest mean difficulty)\n108\t1. abstract_algebra (0.243)\n109\t2. high_school_mathematics (0.266)\n110\t3. global_facts (0.271)\n111\t4. college_mathematics (0.272)\n112\t5. high_school_physics (0.284)\n113\t\n114\t### Easiest (highest mean difficulty)\n115\t1. marketing (0.587)\n116\t2. high_school_government_and_politics (0.582)\n117\t3. us_foreign_policy (0.566)\n118\t4. sociology (0.559)\n119\t5. high_school_psychology (0.555)\n120\t\n121\tItems per subject: min=72 (college_mathematics), max=1,331 (professional_law), median=161.\n122\t\n123\t## Key Answers\n124\t\n125\t### 1. Can we form N≥80 cohorts?\n126\t\n127\t**YES, but only 2 annual cohorts (2023, 2024).** With 63% unclassified models, name-based classification has limited coverage. For finer temporal granularity, external metadata (HF Hub API) would be needed.\n128\t\n129\t### 2. Are there RL-trained models?\n130\t\n131\t**YES.** 477 explicitly labeled (mostly DPO). Many more used RLHF implicitly (Llama-3, Qwen-2, etc.) but aren't detectable from names alone.\n132\t\n133\t### 3. Do RDS files contain IRT parameters?\n134\t\n135\t**YES.** `mmlu-preproc.rds` contains pre-fitted 2PL difficulty and discrimination for all 12,508 items, plus a 5219×12508 response matrix and a 4755/464 train/test split. These can be used directly — no need to re-fit IRT from scratch.\n136\t\n137\t## Artifacts Produced\n138\t\n139\t| File | Description |\n140\t|------|-------------|\n141\t| `metabench_irt_params.csv` | IRT difficulty & discrimination for 12,508 items |\n142\t| `metabench_model_list.txt` | All 5,219 model names |\n143\t| `metabench_model_accuracy.csv` | Per-model overall accuracy |\n144\t| `metabench_subject_stats.csv` | Per-subject item/model counts |\n145\t| `metabench_cohort_analysis.csv` | Cohort sizes and accuracy stats |\n146\t| `metabench_rl_models.csv` | 477 RL-trained models with accuracy |\n147\t| `metabench_summary.json` | Machine-readable comprehensive summary |\n148\t| `metabench_explore.py` | This exploration script |\n149\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d9ac7a36-c57a-461e-ab0e-0412622c1279","uuid":"8b4438ec-6445-4954-9068-d243a313000e","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:53:34.606Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/_project/report/metabench_data_summary.md","content":"# METABENCH MMLU Data Summary\n\n## Dataset Overview\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| Models (CSV) | 5,227 |\n| Models (RDS, after preprocessing) | 5,219 |\n| Items | 12,508 |\n| Subjects | 57 |\n| Total response rows | 73,383,381 |\n| Data format | Long format (source, item, correct), binary 0/1 |\n\nModels per subject ranges 5,225–5,227 (near-complete coverage). 8 models were filtered during METABENCH's preprocessing step (RDS).\n\n## Pre-fitted IRT Parameters (from `mmlu-preproc.rds`)\n\nThe RDS file contains **ready-to-use 2PL IRT parameters**:\n\n| Parameter | Min | Mean | Median | Max |\n|-----------|-----|------|--------|-----|\n| Difficulty (diff) | 0.008 | 0.444 | 0.524 | 0.689 |\n| Discrimination (disc) | -0.551 | 0.076 | 0.107 | 0.291 |\n| SD | 0.106 | — | — | 0.500 |\n\n- **0 items excluded** by METABENCH's quality filter\n- **1,926 items (15.4%)** with negative discrimination — these behave inversely (higher-ability models get them wrong more often)\n- **258 floor items** (empirical p < 0.05), **0 ceiling items** (p > 0.95)\n- Empirical p-value range: [0.011, 0.919]\n\n### Train/Test Split\n\nMETABENCH provides a pre-built split in `mmlu-preproc-split.rds`:\n- **Train**: 4,755 models (91.1%)\n- **Test**: 464 models (8.9%)\n\n## Model Accuracy Distribution\n\n| Statistic | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Mean | 0.592 |\n| Std | 0.160 |\n| Min | 0.218 |\n| Max | 0.911 |\n| P25 | 0.543 |\n| Median | 0.661 |\n| P75 | 0.697 |\n| Below random (0.25) | 366 (7.0%) |\n\nStrong bimodal tendency: most models cluster in 0.54–0.71, with a long left tail of weak/broken models.\n\n## Cohort Analysis (for DIF / temporal grouping)\n\n### By Model Family Classification\n\nOnly **1,929 of 5,219 (37.0%)** models could be classified into known families by regex on model names. The remaining 3,290 are community fine-tunes, merges, and unrecognizable names.\n\n### Annual Cohorts\n\n| Year | N models | N ≥ 80? |\n|------|----------|---------|\n| 2021 | 5 | No |\n| 2022 | 13 | No |\n| 2023 | 1,253 | **Yes** |\n| 2024 | 658 | **Yes** |\n\n**Two annual cohorts meet the N≥80 threshold (2023 and 2024).** However, 2024-H2 only has 2 classified models — the dataset likely predates most 2024-H2 releases. The bulk of classified 2024 models are from H1.\n\n### Top Families\n\n| Family | Count |\n|--------|-------|\n| mistral-0.1 | 400 |\n| llama-3 | 308 |\n| llama-2 | 161 |\n| llama-1 | 135 |\n| mixtral-8x7B | 130 |\n| solar | 92 |\n| zephyr | 85 |\n| qwen-1.5 | 67 |\n| phi-2 | 60 |\n\n### Alternative Cohort Strategy\n\nSince 63% of models are unclassified, **name-based cohort assignment is insufficient for fine-grained temporal analysis**. Consider:\n1. Using accuracy-based quintiles as proxy cohorts\n2. Using the RDS train/test split directly (4755/464)\n3. Cross-referencing with HuggingFace Hub metadata for release dates\n\n## RL-trained Models\n\n**477 models** show RL/alignment signals in their names:\n\n| Method | Count |\n|--------|-------|\n| DPO | 346 |\n| ORPO | 52 |\n| PPO | 11 |\n| KTO | 6 |\n| RLHF | 1 |\n| Reward | 1 |\n\nNote: these are models with explicit RL signals in names. Many models (Llama-3, Qwen-2.5, etc.) used RLHF but don't advertise it in their names. The DPO-labeled models alone provide a substantial pool for RL contamination analysis.\n\n## Subject Difficulty Ranking\n\n### Hardest (lowest mean difficulty)\n1. abstract_algebra (0.243)\n2. high_school_mathematics (0.266)\n3. global_facts (0.271)\n4. college_mathematics (0.272)\n5. high_school_physics (0.284)\n\n### Easiest (highest mean difficulty)\n1. marketing (0.587)\n2. high_school_government_and_politics (0.582)\n3. us_foreign_policy (0.566)\n4. sociology (0.559)\n5. high_school_psychology (0.555)\n\nItems per subject: min=72 (college_mathematics), max=1,331 (professional_law), median=161.\n\n## Key Answers\n\n### 1. Can we form N≥80 cohorts?\n\n**YES, but only 2 annual cohorts (2023, 2024).** With 63% unclassified models, name-based classification has limited coverage. For finer temporal granularity, external metadata (HF Hub API) would be needed.\n\n### 2. Are there RL-trained models?\n\n**YES.** 477 explicitly labeled (mostly DPO). Many more used RLHF implicitly (Llama-3, Qwen-2, etc.) but aren't detectable from names alone.\n\n### 3. Do RDS files contain IRT parameters?\n\n**YES.** `mmlu-preproc.rds` contains pre-fitted 2PL difficulty and discrimination for all 12,508 items, plus a 5219×12508 response matrix and a 4755/464 train/test split. These can be used directly — no need to re-fit IRT from scratch.\n\n## Artifacts Produced\n\n| File | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| `metabench_irt_params.csv` | IRT difficulty & discrimination for 12,508 items |\n| `metabench_model_list.txt` | All 5,219 model names |\n| `metabench_model_accuracy.csv` | Per-model overall accuracy |\n| `metabench_subject_stats.csv` | Per-subject item/model counts |\n| `metabench_cohort_analysis.csv` | Cohort sizes and accuracy stats |\n| `metabench_rl_models.csv` | 477 RL-trained models with accuracy |\n| `metabench_summary.json` | Machine-readable comprehensive summary |\n| `metabench_explore.py` | This exploration script |\n","numLines":149,"startLine":1,"totalLines":149}}}
